    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've definitely kept the chisel sharp—I've been honing it on a strop every few minutes—so I don't think it's bluntness. It feels like the wood just gives way right at the end of the stroke. Do you think it could be the moisture content in the walnut? It's been sitting in my shop for a few weeks, but maybe it's too dry or something?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a great point about the moisture content. Walnut is a hardwood that can be prone to tearing if it's too dry, as it doesn't absorb numerously like softer woods. If the walnut is too dry, it will crack and split more easily at the cut end, leading to tear-outs. Try storing the walnut in a controlled environment to allow it to stabilize in a more optimal moisture range, or consider adjusting your cutting strategy to take into account the possible dryness.",
      "source": "target",
