    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll try letting it acclimate a bit more. One other thing though—the way the fibers seem to just \"burst\" out at the tips feels almost intentional, like something is working against the grain. Is there anything else I should check regarding the grain structure? Maybe something about how the wood was milled or how the fibers are oriented in the boards themselves?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That \"burst\" feel is indeed at odds with the direction of the fibers. Walnut typically has a ring-porous structure, which means the heartwood fibers are oriented in one direction. However, rotationally-sawn boards, like those from rough-sawn lumber, can have uneven grain patterns. Try checking the grain on the boards and ensuring the fibers are aligned intendatively or plane.",
      "source": "target",
